      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Bardstown’s Basilica of Saint Joseph, the first cathedral west of the Appalachians, became the seat of the Diocese of Bardstown in 1808.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        24,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bardstown%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Oscar Getz Museum of Whiskey celebrates Bardstown’s legacy as a bourbon capital, where distilling began in the early 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bardstown%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Railroad history runs through Bardstown, with freight service still running on the former Louisville and Nashville line that once connected the town to the national network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bardstown%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Bardstown’s public library, part of the Nelson County Public Library system, offers a lending collection that reflects the town’s rich literary and historical heritage.",
